
There are four individual test databases (integerIn, integerOut,
analogIn, analogOut, and strings). All of them have a link specified
which assumes a connection through the tnet system. If this is not
what you need then you will have to go into the databases and change
all the link specifications.

I performed the tests via two terminal server ports.
One of the ports is connected, via tnet, to the epics system. The
serial side of the terminal server ports must be connected to each
other with a null modem cable (simply switch tx and rx). I then telnet
to the second terminal server port so that I can type responses by
hand. It is possible to create a task which connects to the port
and generates the responses for you, but bear in mind that there is
no systematic format to the required responses (the record's response
strings were created to either test various formats and/or to demonstrate
those formats). 

If your EPICS system is running on top of Vxworks then you will need
the following line added to your EPICS startup script, substituting
the IP address of your terminal server for all the n's.

	hostAdd ("tserv", "nnn.nnn.nn.nnn");

Then you need to launch tnet
	tnetDevCreate ("/pty/tserv.", "tserv",  port#-here)

If you look in one of the test databases you will see that the link
specification is '/pty/tserv.M' as is required by tnet.



If you want drvAscii to dump information during record init then you
should set drvAsciiDebugLevel, as per the discussion in devAscii.c
header, prior to iocInit. It is worth setting the debug level to 1
and/or 9 at least once, so as to get a feel for the information that
one day you may need to use in order to debug a faulty record.

Once iocInit completes you need to set the 'timeout' record to 7 or
greater. This is the length of time, in seconds, that you will have 
to type in responses when records process. I typically manipulated
records with caput but you could also do so with dbpf.

If you want to see a trace of all the devAscii calls that occur when
a record processes then set the debug level to 32. Setting to 64 will
give a trace of all the drvAscii calls and 96 for both (ie. it is a
bit mask).

I typically set the 'debug' record to 1 so that all transmitted and
received data is displayed. If you set debug to 9 you will also get
a display of all the formatting information that drvAscii is using
for a given record (helps determine if the parm field was parsed as
expected).


It is possible for you to construct your own test database using
whatever link spec you require (for instance /tyCo/1 for a processors
front panel serial port). I often create one for a link such that
the responses are simply echoes of the prompts. This allows electronic 
techs to loopback a serial link at various points to isolate faults.

